t important factor which governs diffusion of drugs across capillaries other than those in the brain is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood flow through the capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipid solubility of the drug"},{"label":"C","text":"pKa value of the drug"},{"label":"D","text":"pH of the medium (p. 13)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a5a844fe151d","stem":"7 Active transport of a substance across biological membranes has the following characteristics except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is specific"},{"label":"B","text":"It is pH dependent"},{"label":"C","text":"It is saturable"},{"label":"D","text":"It requires metabolic energy (p. 13)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-33ca010b1ad6","stem":"8 Tricyclic antidepressants can alter the oral absorption of many drugs by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antidepressant","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complexing with the other drug in the intesti- nal lumen"},{"label":"B","text":"Altering gut motility"},{"label":"C","text":"Altering gut flora"},{"label":"D","text":"Damaging gut mucosa (p. 14, 411)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6cd6ed13a44b","stem":"9 Bioavailability of drug refers to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Percentage of administered dose that reaches systemic circulation in the unchanged form"},{"label":"B","text":"Ratio of oral to parenteral dose"},{"label":"C","text":"Ratio of orally administered drug to that excre- ted in the faeces"},{"label":"D","text":"Ratio of drug excreted unchanged in urine to that excreted as metabolites (p. 15)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5ac894461dc8682397600d64ba38570961371414"},{"id":"q-79d16558d3a4","stem":"10 Bioavailability differences among oral formulations of a drug are most likely to occur if the drug","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is freely water soluble"},{"label":"B","text":"Is completely absorbed"},{"label":"C","text":"Is incompletely absorbed"},{"label":"D","text":"Undergoes little first-pass metabolism (p. 16)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c7ea8d9c96f8","stem":"11 The most important factor governing absorption of a drug from intact skin is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molecular weight of the drug"},{"label":"B","text":"Site of application"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipid solubility of the drug"},{"label":"D","text":"Nature of the base used in the formulation (p. 15)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1c1adda88032","stem":"12 If the total amount of a drug present in the body at a given moment is 2.0 g and its plasma concentration is 25 μg/ml, its volume of distribution is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 L"},{"label":"B","text":"80 L"},{"label":"C","text":"60 L"},{"label":"D","text":"50 L (p. 17)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-13ad10544244","stem":"13 The following attribute of a drug tends to reduce its volume of distribution","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High lipid solubility"},{"label":"B","text":"Low ionisation at physiological pH values"},{"label":"C","text":"High plasma protein binding"},{"label":"D","text":"High tissue binding (p. 17, 18)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2c93b0397111","stem":"14 Marked redistribution is a feature of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Highly lipid soluble drugs"},{"label":"B","text":"Poorly lipid soluble drugs"},{"label":"C","text":"Depot preparations"},{"label":"D","text":"Highly plasma protein bound drugs (p. 17)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"544b16da0064713722f7f0cdcb9949bd39c74c92"},{"id":"q-52265a67f2c9","stem":"15 A nonvolatile, highly lipid soluble drug is metabolized at a rate of 15% per hour. On intravenous injection it produces general anaesthesia for 10 min. Which pro- cess is responsible for termination of its action","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Lipids","topicOriginal":"Lipids","subtopic":"Lipid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolism in liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma protein binding"},{"label":"C","text":"Excretion by kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"Redistribution (p. 17, 342)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Lipids","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6d28a7a421f3","stem":"16 The blood-brain barrier, which restricts entry of many drugs into brain, is constituted by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"P-glycoprotein efflux carriers in brain capillary cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Tight junctions between endothelial cells of brain capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"Enzymes present in brain capillary walls"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above (p. 17, 18)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0a17a1da65c","stem":"17 Which of the following is not true of the blood-brain barrier","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is constituted by tight junctions between the endothelial cells of brain capillaries and the glial tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"It allows passage of lipid soluble drugs into the brain"},{"label":"C","text":"It limits entry of highly ionized drugs into the brain"},{"label":"D","text":"It regulates passage of substances from brain into blood (p. 17, 18)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e4d7e682b21","stem":"18 Weakly acidic drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are bound primarily to α1 acid glycoprotein in plasma"},{"label":"B","text":"Are excreted faster in alkaline urine"},{"label":"C","text":"Are highly ionized in the gastric juice"},{"label":"D","text":"Do not cross blood-brain barrier (p. 18, 26)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"244533c1cfa7407b06321fd0b57181c382cba747"},{"id":"q-0f9ae6d36efc","stem":"19 High plasma protein binding","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases volume of distribution of the drug"},{"label":"B","text":"Facilitates glomerular filtration of the drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Minimises drug interactions"},{"label":"D","text":"Generally makes the drug long acting (p. 18)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5f0a591d0008","stem":"20 The plasma protein bound fraction of a drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contributes to the response at the given moment"},{"label":"B","text":"Remains constant irrespective of the total drug concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"Remains constant irrespective of the disease state"},{"label":"D","text":"Is not available for metabolism unless actively extracted by the liver (p. 18, 19)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-46c61612c172","stem":"21 Biotransformation of drugs is primarily directed to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activate the drug"},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivate the drug"},{"label":"C","text":"Convert lipid soluble drugs into nonlipid solu- ble metabolites"},{"label":"D","text":"Convert nonlipid soluble drugs into lipid solu- ble metabolites (p. 20)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a2f99791bccb","stem":"22 Which of the following is a prodrug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydralazine"},{"label":"B","text":"Clonidine"},{"label":"C","text":"Captopril"},{"label":"D","text":"Enalapril (p. 20)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a96a6612fafe","stem":"23 A prodrug is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The prototype member of a class of drugs"},{"label":"B","text":"The oldest member of a class of drugs"},{"label":"C","text":"An inactive drug that is transformed in the body to an active metabolite"},{"label":"D","text":"A drug that is stored in body tissues and is then gradually released in the circulation (p. 20)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b9f773626b0","stem":"24 Which of the following cytochrome P450 isoenzymes is involved in the metabolism of largest number of drugs in human beings and has been implicated in some dangerous drug interactions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"CYP 3A4"},{"label":"B","text":"CYP 2C9"},{"label":"C","text":"CYP 2E1"},{"label":"D","text":"CYP 1A2 (p. 21, 142)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f8699884fa11","stem":"25 The following is not true of the cytochrome P450 isoenzyme CYP2D6","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It generates the hepatotoxic metabolite N-acetyl benzoquinone immine from paracetamol"},{"label":"B","text":"It is involved in demethylation of codeine into morphine"},{"label":"C","text":"Its altered form is responsible for poor capacity to hydroxylate many drugs including metoprolol"},{"label":"D","text":"It is inhibited by quinidine (p. 21, 23)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7b843c4461c4","stem":"26 The most commonly occurring conjugation reaction for drugs and their metabolites is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucuronidation"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetylation"},{"label":"C","text":"Methylation"},{"label":"D","text":"Glutathione conjugation (p. 22)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-84f13bcf548d","stem":"27 Microsomal enzyme induction can be a cause of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolerance"},{"label":"B","text":"Physical dependence"},{"label":"C","text":"Psychological dependence"},{"label":"D","text":"Idiosyncrasy"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"27 Microsomal enzyme induction can be a cause of","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolerance"},{"label":"B","text":"Physical dependence"},{"label":"C","text":"Psychological dependence"},{"label":"D","text":"Idiosyncrasy (p. 24)"}]}},{"id":"q-81d62632b13e","stem":"28 The following drug metabolizing reaction is entirely nonmicrosomal","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucuronide conjugation"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetylation"},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidation"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduction (p. 23)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"1b211b2e0240ffab56b81cffb5fd4aa95bf364e5"},{"id":"q-e754043659d1","stem":"29 Which of the following types of drug metabolizing enzymes are inducible","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsomal enzymes"},{"label":"B","text":"Nonmicrosomal enzymes"},{"label":"C","text":"Both microsomal and nonmicrosomal enzymes"},{"label":"D","text":"Mitochondrial enzymes (p. 23, 24)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"independent_provisional_reasoning","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cfcd23010ffe","stem":"30 Induction of drug metabolizing enzymes involves","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A conformational change in the enzyme protein to favour binding of substrate molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"Expression of enzyme molecules on the surface of hepatocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Enhanced transport of substrate molecules into hepatocytes"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased synthesis of enzyme protein (p. 24)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"independent_provisional_reasoning","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ffc6e835935","stem":"31 Select the drug that undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism in the liver","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenobarbitone"},{"label":"B","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Phenylbutazone"},{"label":"D","text":"Theophylline (p. 25)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-30f31e0a5659","stem":"32 Drugs which undergo high degree of first-pass meta- bolism in liver","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Have low oral bioavailability"},{"label":"B","text":"Are excreted primarily in bile"},{"label":"C","text":"Are contraindicated in liver disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Exhibit zero order kinetics of elimination (p. 25)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0d7ab134ebed","stem":"33 Glomerular filtration of a drug is affected by its","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipid solubility"},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma protein binding"},{"label":"C","text":"Degree of ionization"},{"label":"D","text":"Rate of tubular secretion (p. 26)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"602b1a13cbf0f4d3865a55a3809af921ba6f4b17"},{"id":"q-bed8db172cde","stem":"34 If a drug undergoes net tubular secretion, its renal clearance will be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More than the glomerular filtration rate"},{"label":"B","text":"Equal to the glomerular filtration rate"},{"label":"C","text":"Less than the glomerular filtration rate"},{"label":"D","text":"Equal to the rate of urine formation (p. 27)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-525bfc697b34","stem":"35 The plasma half life of penicillin-G is longer in the new born because their","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma protein level is low"},{"label":"B","text":"Drug metabolizing enzymes are immature"},{"label":"C","text":"Glomerular filtration rate is low"},{"label":"D","text":"Tubular transport mechanisms are not well developed (p. 27)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b6af2de6541","stem":"36 If a drug is excreted in urine at the rate of 10 mg/hr at a steady-state plasma concentration of 5 mg/L, then its renal clearance is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 L/hr"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 L/hr"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 L/hr"},{"label":"D","text":"20 L/hr (p. 27)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a0b32d05d837","stem":"37 Which of the following is not a primary/fundamental, but a derived pharmacokinetic parameter","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Pharmacokinetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bioavailability"},{"label":"B","text":"Volume of distribution"},{"label":"C","text":"Clearance"},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma half life (p. 29)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7bef8e9be2fc","stem":"38 If a drug is eliminated by first order kinetics","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A constant amount of the drug will be elimi- nated per unit time"},{"label":"B","text":"Its clearance value will remain constant"},{"label":"C","text":"Its elimination half life will increase with dose"},{"label":"D","text":"It will be completely eliminated from the body in 2 × half life period (p. 27-28)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"62f90e5301a147d38663fabe25d14b768c56cbb5"},{"id":"q-d4bd3f3be9b5","stem":"39 If a drug has a constant bioavailability and first order elimination, its maintenance dose rate will be directly proportional to its","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Volume of distribution"},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma protein binding"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipid solubility"},{"label":"D","text":"Total body clearance (p. 29)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dbb92b94ff7f","stem":"40 If the clearance of a drug remains constant, doubling the dose rate will increase the steady-state plasma drug concentration by a factor of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"× 3"},{"label":"B","text":"× 2"},{"label":"C","text":"× 1.5"},{"label":"D","text":"× 1.3 (p. 29)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88a6f2b37b30","stem":"41 When the same dose of a drug is repeated at half life intervals, the steady-state (plateau) plasma drug con- centration is reached after","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2–3 half lives"},{"label":"B","text":"4–5 half lives"},{"label":"C","text":"6–7 half lives"},{"label":"D","text":"8–10 half lives (p. 30)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ce32160eaeb","stem":"42 The loading dose of a drug is governed by its","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal clearance"},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma half life"},{"label":"C","text":"Volume of distribution"},{"label":"D","text":"Elimination rate constant (p. 30)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4126f88e5595","stem":"43 Monitoring of blood levels of diuretic drugs is not practised because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Diuretic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"No sensitive methods for measuring blood levels of diuretics are available"},{"label":"B","text":"It is easier to measure the effect of these drugs"},{"label":"C","text":"Response to diuretics is not related to their blood levels"},{"label":"D","text":"Diuretics need activation in the body (p. 31)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ae98ecf39d21c342898aeb7b97b75108964762ea"},{"id":"q-4f2fabf14228","stem":"44 Monitoring plasma drug concentration is useful while using","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antihypertensive drugs"},{"label":"B","text":"Levodopa"},{"label":"C","text":"Lithium carbonate"},{"label":"D","text":"MAO inhibitors (p. 31)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-32a6e51def0c","stem":"45 Sustained/controlled release oral dosage form is appropriate for the following type of drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"An antiarthritic with a plasma half life of 24 hr"},{"label":"B","text":"A sleep inducing hypnotic with a plasma half life of 2 hours"},{"label":"C","text":"An antihypertensive with a plasma half life of 3 hours"},{"label":"D","text":"An analgesic with a plasma half life of 6 hours used for relief of casual headache (p. 31)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-803420860017","stem":"46 Microsomal enzyme induction has one of the following features","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Takes about one week to develop"},{"label":"B","text":"Results in increased affinity of the enzyme for the substrate"},{"label":"C","text":"It is irreversible"},{"label":"D","text":"Can be used to treat acute drug poisonings (p. 24, 34, 35)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"independent_provisional_reasoning","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f52b11317e24","stem":"1 Which of the following drugs acts by inhibiting an enzyme in the body","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Allopurinol"},{"label":"C","text":"Levodopa"},{"label":"D","text":"Metoclopramide (p. 34)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a898e67c259","stem":"2 The following is a competitive type of enzyme inhibitor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetazolamide"},{"label":"B","text":"Disulfiram"},{"label":"C","text":"Physostigmine"},{"label":"D","text":"Theophylline (p. 34, 35)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d076d85009def0f81f0980535a76fac0752cd84d"},{"id":"q-21a1e563c144","stem":"3 What is true in relation to drug receptors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All drugs act through specific receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"All drug receptors are located on the surface of the target cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Agonists induce a conformational change in the receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"Partial agonists have low affinity for the receptor (p. 35-37)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4c837b1c6316","stem":"4 Drugs acting through receptors exhibit the following features except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Structural specificity"},{"label":"B","text":"High potency"},{"label":"C","text":"Competitive antagonism"},{"label":"D","text":"Dependence of action on lipophilicity (p. 45)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ba5870cfa3a0","stem":"5 Study of drug-receptor interaction has now shown that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximal response occurs only when all recep- tors are occupied by the drug"},{"label":"B","text":"Drugs exert an ‘all or none’ action on a receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Receptor and drugs acting on it have rigid complementary ‘lock and key’ structural features"},{"label":"D","text":"Properties of ‘affinity’ and ‘intrinsic activity’ are independently variable(p. 36-37)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c83c19ff7ce0","stem":"6 A partial agonist can antagonise the effects of a full agonist because it has","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"High affinity but low intrinsic activity"},{"label":"B","text":"Low affinity but high intrinsic activity"},{"label":"C","text":"No affinity and low intrinsic activity"},{"label":"D","text":"High affinity but no intrinsic activity (p. 37)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-88ac8b898051","stem":"7 Receptor agonists possess","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Affinity but no intrinsic activity"},{"label":"B","text":"Intrinsic activity but no affinity"},{"label":"C","text":"Affinity and intrinsic activity with a + sign"},{"label":"D","text":"Affinity and intrinsic activity with a – sign (p. 37)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7f4931df422ac5502f219967b8a915656a40437b"},{"id":"q-8ceae329ea99","stem":"8 Agonists affect the receptor molecule in the following manner","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alter its amino acid sequence"},{"label":"B","text":"Denature the receptor protein"},{"label":"C","text":"Alter its folding or alignment of subunits"},{"label":"D","text":"Induce covalent bond formation (p. 38)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6d5a7a9a02c8","stem":"9 Receptors perform the following function/functions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligand recognition"},{"label":"B","text":"Signal transduction"},{"label":"C","text":"Both ligand recognition and signal transduction"},{"label":"D","text":"Disposal of agonists and antagonists (p. 45)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-212b0fac4e8d","stem":"10 The following receptor type has 7 helical membrane spanning amino acid segments with 3 extracellular and 3 intracellular loops","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyrosine protein kinase receptor"},{"label":"B","text":"Gene expression regulating receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Intrinsic ion channel containing receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"G protein coupled receptor (p. 39-40)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e050e9f26232","stem":"11 Which of the following is a G protein coupled recep- tor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscarinic cholinergic receptor"},{"label":"B","text":"Nicotinic cholinergic receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Glucocorticoid receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin receptor (p. 40, 42)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-204752e169cb","stem":"12 The following receptor has an intrinsic ion channel","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histamine H1 receptor"},{"label":"B","text":"Histamine H2 receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenergic alfa receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"GABA-benzodiazepine receptor (p. 42)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ce16dc58eccb726d74b0261c376238cb273c653b"},{"id":"q-3d3bb8140191","stem":"13 Select the receptor that is located intracellularly","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opioid μ receptor"},{"label":"B","text":"Steroid receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Prostaglandin receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"Angiotensin receptor (p. 42, 43)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-42c36964669a","stem":"14 Agonist induced autophosphorylation, internalization and down regulation is a distinctive feature of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"G-protein coupled receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Intrinsic ion channel containing receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Tyrosine protein kinase receptors"},{"label":"D","text":"Receptors regulating gene expression (p. 43)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f541e0737e7d","stem":"15 All of the following subserve as intracellular second messengers in receptor mediated signal transduction except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclic AMP"},{"label":"B","text":"Inositol trisphosphate"},{"label":"C","text":"Diacyl glycerols"},{"label":"D","text":"G proteins (p. 39-42)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b6d9bcf7a79d","stem":"16 The receptor transduction mechanism with the fastest time-course of response effectuation is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenylyl cyclase-cyclic AMP pathway"},{"label":"B","text":"Phospholipase C-IP3: DAG pathway"},{"label":"C","text":"Intrinsic ion channel operation"},{"label":"D","text":"Protein synthesis modulation (p. 43)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b5b27a2def33","stem":"17 A receptor which itself has enzymatic property is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin receptor"},{"label":"B","text":"Progesterone receptor"},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"Glucagon receptor (p. 43, 239)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a091dd9c426f7f3fead8091c95880cf545ccc0ed"},{"id":"q-e326abf20166","stem":"18 Down regulation of receptors can occur as a consequence of","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous use of agonists"},{"label":"B","text":"Continuous use of antagonists"},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic use of CNS depressants"},{"label":"D","text":"Denervation (p. 43, 45)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-12b9c0396d5f","stem":"19 The following statement is not true of log dose-response curve","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is almost linear except at the ends"},{"label":"B","text":"It is a rectangular hyperbola"},{"label":"C","text":"It facilitates comparison of different agonists"},{"label":"D","text":"It can help in discriminating between competitive and noncompetitive antagonists (p. 46)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-15b9a608b853","stem":"20 When therapeutic effects decline both below and above a narrow range of doses, a drug is said to exhibit","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceiling effect"},{"label":"B","text":"Desensitization"},{"label":"C","text":"Therapeutic window phenomenon"},{"label":"D","text":"Nonreceptor mediated action (p. 46)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5782475a0ca","stem":"21 Which of the following drugs exhibits ‘therapeutic window’ phenomenon","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Captopril"},{"label":"B","text":"Furosemide"},{"label":"C","text":"Diazepam"},{"label":"D","text":"Imipramine (p. 46, 410)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"25a90d194008a567c45a51f0a99a3c34aa03251d"},{"id":"q-12d63f983cc3","stem":"22 The following statement is not true of ‘potency’ of a drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refers to the dose of the drug needed to produce a certain degree of response"},{"label":"B","text":"Can be related to that of its congeners by the relative position of its dose-response curve on the dose axis"},{"label":"C","text":"It is often not a major consideration in the choice of a drug"},{"label":"D","text":"It reflects the capacity of the drug to produce a drastic response (p. 47)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c351e061d65e","stem":"23 ‘Drug efficacy’ refers to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The range of diseases in which the drug is beneficial"},{"label":"B","text":"The maximal intensity of response that can be produced by the drug"},{"label":"C","text":"The dose of the drug needed to produce half maximal effect"},{"label":"D","text":"The dose of the drug needed to produce thera- peutic effect (p. 47)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05e5c20e65e4","stem":"24 Which of the following is always true","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A more potent drug is more efficacious"},{"label":"B","text":"A more potent drug is safer"},{"label":"C","text":"A more potent drug is clinically superior"},{"label":"D","text":"A more potent drug can produce the same response at lower doses (p. 47)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5036022a4014","stem":"25 Higher efficacy of a drug necessarily confers","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Greater safety"},{"label":"B","text":"Therapeutic superiority"},{"label":"C","text":"Capacity to produce more intense response"},{"label":"D","text":"Cost saving (p. 47)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8be18bcb2fc6d8aca38fdfff201f1f2ddfbb473a"},{"id":"q-32bf5e7492b4","stem":"26 If the dose-response curves of a drug for producing different actions are widely separated on the dose axis, the drug is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Highly potent"},{"label":"B","text":"Highly efficacious"},{"label":"C","text":"Highly toxic"},{"label":"D","text":"Highly selective (p. 47-48)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-79e68b012d45","stem":"27 The therapeutic index of a drug is a measure of its","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safety"},{"label":"B","text":"Potency"},{"label":"C","text":"Efficacy"},{"label":"D","text":"Dose variability (p. 48)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4015274366c3","stem":"28 Compared to the drug named within parenthesis, which of the following drugs has a higher potency but lower efficacy","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pethidine (morphine)"},{"label":"B","text":"Furosemide (hydrochlorothiazide)"},{"label":"C","text":"Diazepam (pentobarbitone)"},{"label":"D","text":"Enalapril (captopril) (p. 47)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8470093945b1","stem":"29 If the effect of combination of two drugs is equal to the sum of their individual effects, the two drugs are exhibiting","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Potentiation"},{"label":"B","text":"Synergism"},{"label":"C","text":"Cross tolerance"},{"label":"D","text":"Antagonism (p. 49)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-60b72166eee5","stem":"30 The antagonism between adrenaline and histamine is called ‘physiological antagonism’ because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Physiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both are physiologically present in the body"},{"label":"B","text":"They act on physiological receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Both affect many physiological processes"},{"label":"D","text":"They have opposite physiological effects (p. 49)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Physiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6f4b18cf7a95eae77b6ab6774f01f1abd942db92"},{"id":"q-771b9cfe58d8","stem":"31 The antidotal action of sodium nitrite in cyanide poison- ing is based on","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physical antagonism"},{"label":"B","text":"Chemical antagonism"},{"label":"C","text":"Physiological antagonism"},{"label":"D","text":"Noncompetitive antagonism (p. 49, 492)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2df5f6b65d01","stem":"32 A drug ‘R’ producing no response by itself causes the log dose-response curve of another drug ‘S’ to shift to the right in a parallel manner without decreasing the maximal response: Drug ‘R’ is a","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial agonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Inverse agonist"},{"label":"C","text":"Competitive antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Noncompetitive antagonist (p. 50)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eacef1aa115a","stem":"33 A drug which does not produce any action by itself but decreases the slope of the log dose-response curve and suppresses the maximal response to another drug is a","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physiological antagonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Competitive antagonist"},{"label":"C","text":"Noncompetitive antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Partial agonist (p. 50)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0acf72032b89","stem":"34 The following is not a feature of competitive antagonists","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemical resemblance with the agonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Parallel rightward shift of the agonist log dose- response curve"},{"label":"C","text":"Suppression of maximal agonist response"},{"label":"D","text":"Apparent reduction in agonist affinity for the receptor (p. 50)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c349bc0033a6","stem":"35 The following is a competitive antagonist of GABA but a noncompetitive antagonist of diazepam","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Picrotoxin"},{"label":"B","text":"Muscimol"},{"label":"C","text":"Flumazenil"},{"label":"D","text":"Bicuculline (p. 50, 364, 435)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dff0d1128019fb5260f12faff8ee6cd025e8631d"},{"id":"q-eca2b1e58b64","stem":"36 The dose of the following class of drugs has to be adjusted by repeated measurement of the affected physiological parameter","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral contraceptives"},{"label":"B","text":"Antiepileptics"},{"label":"C","text":"Antidepressants"},{"label":"D","text":"Oral anticoagulants (p. 51)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d8f1148059d4","stem":"37 A drug which is generally administered in standard doses without the need for dose individualization is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Mebendazole"},{"label":"C","text":"Prednisolone"},{"label":"D","text":"Digoxin (p. 51)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f43f40ac8960","stem":"38 Which of the following statements is not true of fixed dose combination formulations","type":"Calculation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are more convenient"},{"label":"B","text":"Contraindication to one of the components does not contraindicate the formulation"},{"label":"C","text":"The dose of any one component cannot be independently adjusted"},{"label":"D","text":"The time course of action of the different compo- nents may not be identical (p. 51-52)"}],"priority":2,"status":