actions of atropine is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric glands"},{"label":"B","text":"Salivary glands"},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary bladder muscle"},{"label":"D","text":"Heart (p. 95)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-05892d19f747","stem":"5 Hyoscine differs from atropine in that it","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exerts depressant effects on the CNS at relati- vely low doses"},{"label":"B","text":"Exerts more potent effects on the heart than on the eye"},{"label":"C","text":"Is longer acting"},{"label":"D","text":"Has weaker antimotion sickness activity (p. 96)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9542804debdf","stem":"6 The quarternary analogues of belladonna alkaloids are preferred over the natural alkaloids for antisecretory/ antispasmodic indications because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They have additional nicotinic receptor block- ing activity"},{"label":"B","text":"They are incompletely absorbed after oral administration"},{"label":"C","text":"They are devoid of CNS and ocular effects"},{"label":"D","text":"Dose to dose they are more potent than atropine (p. 107) (Note: Many quarternary anticholinergics do have addi- tional nicotinic blocking activity and because of high ionization they are incompletely absorbed. But the reason for preferring them is lack of central and ocular effects. Most compounds are dose to dose less potent than atropine.)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"1334d35312788934521277f0a5e568c61fccb4e7"},{"id":"q-7b1dbf10585d","stem":"7 Inhaled ipratropium bromide has the following advan- tages except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It does not alter respiratory secretions"},{"label":"B","text":"It does not depress airway mucociliary clearance"},{"label":"C","text":"It has faster onset of bronchodilator action than inhaled salbutamol"},{"label":"D","text":"It only rarely produces systemic side effects (p. 97)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-de4f98283aa5","stem":"8 Which of the following anticholinergic drugs is primarily used in preanaesthetic medication and during surgery","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycopyrrolate"},{"label":"B","text":"Pipenzolate methyl bromide"},{"label":"C","text":"Isopropamide"},{"label":"D","text":"Dicyclomine (p. 97)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82e3b2c8b445","stem":"9 Children are more susceptible than adults to the following action of atropine","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tachycardia producing"},{"label":"B","text":"Cycloplegic"},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric antisecretory"},{"label":"D","text":"Central excitant and hyperthermic (p. 94, 95, 99)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f082a629569","stem":"10 Glycopyrrolate is the preferred antimuscarinic drug for use before and during surgery because","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is potent and fast acting"},{"label":"B","text":"It has no central action"},{"label":"C","text":"It has antisecretory and vagolytic actions"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above (p. 97, 98)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-24698b15d0a0","stem":"11 Choose the relatively vasicoselective anticholinergic drug used for urinary frequency and urge incontinence due to detrusor instability","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pirenzepine"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxybutynin"},{"label":"C","text":"Oxyphenonium"},{"label":"D","text":"Glycopyrolate (p. 97, 98)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b5ea7b9ae6ae170a81d0f03cffbb4788127f8966"},{"id":"q-06736742f306","stem":"12 Which of the following mydriatics has the fastest and briefest action","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atropine"},{"label":"B","text":"Homatropine"},{"label":"C","text":"Tropicamide"},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclopentolate (p. 98)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["top