ate limiting enzyme in the synthesis of catecho- lamines is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyrosine hydroxylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa decarboxylase"},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine β-hydroxylase"},{"label":"D","text":"Noradrenaline N-methyl transferase (p. 103)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"independent_provisional_reasoning","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-061cad712934","stem":"3 The most efficacious inhibitor of catecholamine syn- thesis in the body is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-methyl-p-tyrosine"},{"label":"B","text":"α-methyldopa"},{"label":"C","text":"α-methyl-norepinephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Entacapone (p. 103, 107)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b8ff1e60058","stem":"4 Tyramine induces release of noradrenaline from adre- nergic nerve endings","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"By depolarizing the axonal membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"By mobilizing Ca2+"},{"label":"C","text":"By a nonexocytotic process"},{"label":"D","text":"Only in the presence of MAO inhibitors (p. 104)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b2994634b875","stem":"5 The following type/types of noradrenaline uptake is blocked by reserpine","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axonal uptake"},{"label":"B","text":"Granular uptake"},{"label":"C","text":"Extraneuronal uptake"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above (p. 104)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d4d092dc2de1","stem":"6 The principal process which terminates the action of noradrenaline released from adrenergic nerve ending is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degradation by MAO"},{"label":"B","text":"Methylation by COMT"},{"label":"C","text":"Axonal uptake"},{"label":"D","text":"Extraneuronal uptake (p. 104)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a1afc73b6fb89c3db57c6731325cfa6e7bd18b5e"},{"id":"q-bd75f218980a","stem":"7 Which of the following is not the basis for subclassi- fying β adrenergic receptors into β1 and β2","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selectivity of agonists"},{"label":"B","text":"Selectivity of antagonists"},{"label":"C","text":"Transducer pathway of response effectuation"},{"label":"D","text":"Organ selective location (p. 105, 106) (Note: Both β1 and β2 adrenoceptors utilize the adenylyl cyclase-cyclic AMP pathway.)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-57ed79c43887","stem":"8 The β3 adrenoceptor differs from the other subtypes of β receptor in that it","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is not blocked by the conventional doses of propranolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Is located primarily in the heart"},{"label":"C","text":"Regulates blood sugar level"},{"label":"D","text":"Is not coupled to G proteins (p. 106)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f5ef5a22882","stem":"9 The α2 adrenoceptors are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Located exclusively on the adrenergic nerve endings"},{"label":"B","text":"Prejunctional, postjunctional as well as extra- junctional in location"},{"label":"C","text":"Selectively activated by phenylephrine"},{"label":"D","text":"Selectively blocked by clonidine (p. 106)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b4f7c11b470f","stem":"10 The following is a selective α2 adrenoceptor antagonist","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prazosin"},{"label":"B","text":"Phentolamine"},{"label":"C","text":"Yohimbine"},{"label":"D","text":"Clonidine (p. 106, 122)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"0d2b21dd50628dc26dfe285a8ca59631246ac8b3"},{"id":"q-0b7898ce1dd3","stem":"11 A sympathomimetic amine that acts almost exclu- sively by releasing noradrenaline from the nerve endings is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Upper Limb","topicOriginal":"Upper Limb","subtopic":"Arm","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ephedrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Dopamine"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoprenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"Tyramine (p. 106, 107)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Upper Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0bf5bceaf14e","stem":"12 The following sympathomimetic amine has agonistic action on α1 + α2 + β1 + β3 adrenoceptors, but not on β2 receptors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"B","text":"Noradrenaline"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoprenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine (p. 107)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e4a2d719c929","stem":"13 The following action of adrenaline is mediated by both α and β receptors producing the same directional effect","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac stimulation"},{"label":"B","text":"Intestinal relaxation"},{"label":"C","text":"Dilatation of pupil"},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchodilatation (p. 109)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-298df6d2fba0","stem":"14 The following action of adrenaline is not mediated by β receptors","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilatation of blood vessels"},{"label":"B","text":"Dilatation of pupil"},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchodilation"},{"label":"D","text":"Renin release from kidney (p. 109, 110)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-db96a22404e8","stem":"15 Low doses of adrenaline dilate the following vascular bed","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cutaneous"},{"label":"B","text":"Mucosal"},{"label":"C","text":"Renal"},{"label":"D","text":"Skeletal muscle (p. 109, 111)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6158c77a0c4db1ea386b580c9e6f57c8e1f5b424"},{"id":"q-52148d7e6973","stem":"16 Vasomotor reversal phenomenon after administration of an α adrenergic blocker is seen with","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"B","text":"Noradrenaline"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoprenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above drugs (p. 110, 119)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d84c58d03fd","stem":"17 Adrenergic β2 agonists produce muscle tremor by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facilitating neuromuscular transmission"},{"label":"B","text":"Incomplete fusion of contractile response of individual fibres"},{"label":"C","text":"Enhanced firing of muscle spindles"},{"label":"D","text":"Both (b) and (c) are correct (p. 110)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-21aeecc8d5a4","stem":"18 Adrenaline is inactive orally because it is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Not absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract"},{"label":"B","text":"Destroyed by gastric acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Completely metabolized in the intestinal mucosa and liver before reaching systemic circulation"},{"label":"D","text":"Taken up by adrenergic nerve endings of the intestinal wall, liver and lungs (p. 111)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c6ea5041a2ce","stem":"19 Adrenaline raises blood glucose level by the following actions except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inducing hepatic glycogenolysis"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibiting insulin secretion from pancreatic β cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Augmenting glucagon secretion from pan- creatic α cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibiting peripheral glucose utilization (p. 110-111)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ea39ecc59814","stem":"20 The metabolic actions of adrenaline include the follo- wing except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Metabolism · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Metabolism","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycogenolysis in liver and muscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of neoglucogenesis in liver"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipolysis in adipose tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"Release of potassium from liver followed by its uptake (p. 111)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Metabolism · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_provisional_reasoning","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8d9b2d2de22d39bc6eea250d18b8a6c2da793ade"},{"id":"q-9e8a71e528e9","stem":"21 Noradrenaline is administered by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subcutaneous injection"},{"label":"B","text":"Intramuscular injection"},{"label":"C","text":"Slow intravenous infusion"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above routes (p. 111)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d0318855ba4a","stem":"22 Dopaminergic D1 and D2 as well as adrenergic α and β1, but not β2 receptors are activated by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Dobutamine"},{"label":"C","text":"Methoxamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine (p. 112)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-60f3a030abd3","stem":"23 Dobutamine differs from dopamine in that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology",