,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b4681fd2e31aad765bc9870f82f91ab2c259c74f"},{"id":"q-403f78287ff7","stem":"25 In a patient of hypertension, the dose of propranolol that normalized blood pressure, reduced resting heart rate to 50/min. Which of the following β blockers will be most suitable for him as an alternative so that heart rate is not markedly reduced","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pindolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Celiprolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Bisoprolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Atenolol (p. 129)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-31131c105982","stem":"26 In patients of congestive heart failure, β-adrenergic blockers","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Heart","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are absolutely contraindicated"},{"label":"B","text":"Can prolong survival"},{"label":"C","text":"Can improve haemodynamics after compen- sation has been restored"},{"label":"D","text":"Both B and C are correct (p. 130, 469)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f81ee86de8e8","stem":"27 The basis for use of β-adrenergic blockers in congestive heart failure (CHF) is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Heart","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They exert positive inotropic effect in CHF"},{"label":"B","text":"They counteract deleterious effect of sympa- thetic overactivity on the myocardium"},{"label":"C","text":"They exert antiischaemic effect on the heart"},{"label":"D","text":"They prevent cardiac arrhythmias (p. 130, 469)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f720a55783f8","stem":"28 Adrenergic β1 selective blockers offer the following advantages except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower propensity to cause bronchospasm"},{"label":"B","text":"Less prone to produce cold hands and feet as side effect"},{"label":"C","text":"Withdrawal is less likely to exacerbate angina pectoris"},{"label":"D","text":"Less liable to impair exercise capacity (p. 127-128)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e2046141a72c1a7bdba0f6521d443488ddd04e7f"},{"id":"q-f77aac372d86","stem":"29 The following is not a feature of cardioselective beta blockers, when compared to propranolol","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are ineffective in suppressing muscle tremor"},{"label":"B","text":"They are safer in diabetics"},{"label":"C","text":"They are less likely to cause bradycardia"},{"label":"D","text":"They are less likely to worsen Raynaud’s disease (p. 128)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c047b9eaf9a8","stem":"30 Select the β adrenergic blocker that is primarily eliminated unchanged by renal excretion","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"B","text":"Metoprolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Esmolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Atenolol (p. 128, 129)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-36dcf0922a47","stem":"31 In a patient of myocardial infarction, β adrenergic blockers are used with the following aim/aims","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"To reduce the incidence of reinfarction"},{"label":"B","text":"To prevent cardiac arrhythmias"},{"label":"C","text":"To limit size of the infarct"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above (p. 130)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f9009750222","stem":"32 Select the β-adrenergic blocker that has additional α1 blocking, vasodilator and antioxidant properties","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carvedilol"},{"label":"B","text":"Celiprolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Acebutolol"},{"label":"D","text":"Metoprolol (p. 131)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a7c1d3a85de9","stem":"33 In hyperthyroidism, β adrenergic blockers are used","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"To induce euthyroid state"},{"label":"B","text":"As definitive therapy"},{"label":"C","text":"For rapid control of certain symptoms while awaiting response to carbimazole"},{"label":"D","text":"To reduce basal metabolic rate (p. 130, 234)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"623ca69dacb2ac0b9b061a4263fcd07de8f92909"},{"id":"q-fc8d1234bcc6","stem":"34 Select the drug that suppresses essential tremor, but not parkinsonian tremor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Procyclidine"},{"label":"B","text":"Propranolol"},{"label":"C","text":"Promethazine"},{"label":"D","text":"Prochlorperazine (p. 131)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8aca4b5af165","stem":"35 Labetalol has","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"More potent β adrenergic blocking than α blocking activity"},{"label":"B","text":"More potent α adrenergic blocking than β blocking activity"},{"label":"C","text":"Equal α and β adrenergic blocking activity"},{"label":"D","text":"β1 agonistic activity in addition to α and β adrenergic blockade (p. 131)"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b81af3dd57c7","stem":"36 Labetalol differs from propranolol in that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has additional α1 blocking property"},{"label":"B","text":"It is a selective β1 blocker"},{"label":"C","text":"It does not undergo first pass metabolism"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above (p. 131)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6efb34e0c386f5e2aa76632f057439f805c8cc52"},{"id":"q-e464e2cdaa9b","stem":"1 Autacoids differ from hormones in that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autacoids are involved only in the causation of pathological states"},{"label":"B","text":"Autacoids do not have a specific cell/tissue of origin"},{"label":"C","text":"Autacoids generally act locally at the site of generation and release"},{"label":"D","text":"Both ‘B’ and ‘C’ are correct (p. 134)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-354e372a6eb9","stem":"2 Which of the following is a selective H1 receptor agonist","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"4-methyl histamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Impromidine"},{"label":"C","text":"2-Thiazolyl ethylamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorpheniramine (p. 137)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cb1ef250764b","stem":"3 The action of histamine that is not mediated through H1 receptors is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Release of EDRF from vascular endothelium resulting in vasodilatation"},{"label":"B","text":"Direct action on vascular smooth muscle causing vasodilatation"},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchoconstriction"},{"label":"D","text":"Release of catecholamines from adrenal medulla (p. 136, 137) 11111 00000 ..... 11111 D 11111 00000 ..... 22222 C 11111 00000 ..... 33333 B 6262626262 MCQs in Pharmacology"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"dbdd3aa8a4657cfdd455fa852fc42df932bce82a"},{"id":"q-0b5193f8aba1","stem":"4 Histamine exerts the following actions except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilatation of large blood vessels"},{"label":"B","text":"Dilatation of small blood vessels"},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulation of isolated guineapig heart"},{"label":"D","text":"Itching (p. 136)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0b970f77a376","stem":"5 Fall in blood pressure caused by larger doses of histamine is blocked by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"H1 antihistaminics alone"},{"label":"B","text":"H2 antagonists alone"},{"label":"C","text":"Combination of H1 and H2 antagonists"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{"sourceStem":"5 Fall in blood pressure caused by larger doses of histamine is blocked by","sourceOptions":[{"label":"A","text":"H1 antihistaminics alone"},{"label":"B","text":"H2 antagonists alone"},{"label":"C","text":"Combination of H1 and H2 antagonists"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above (p. 136)"}]}},{"id":"q-4e253d3aab0d","stem":"6 The following statement about histamine is not correct","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the sole mediator of immediate hyper- sensitivity reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"It plays no role in delayed hypersensitivity reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"It serves as a neurotransmitter in the brain"},{"label":"D","text":"All types of histamine receptors are G protein coupled receptors (p. 137, 138)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eeebfb348267","stem":"7 Histamine is involved as a mediator in the following pathological condition","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delayed hypersensitivity reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Inflammation"},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinoid syndrome"},{"label":"D","text":"Variant angina (p. 138)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c472e7f0649a","stem":"8 The drug that can directly release histamine from mast cells without involving antigen-antibody reac- tion is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Immunology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirin"},{"label":"B","text":"Procaine"},{"label":"C","text":"Morphine"},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfadiazine (p. 138) 11111 00000 ..... 44444 A 11111 00000 ..... 55555 C 11111 00000 ..... 66666 A 11111 00000 ..... 77777 B 11111 00000 ..... 88888 C Autacoids 6363636363"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Immunology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"e9ce17a88b378a8ce4ce1fadcaf6add854c8ed49"},{"id":"q-864e1023154a","stem":"9 High anticholinergic property is present in the following antihistaminic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diphenhydramine"},{"label":"B","text":"Astemizole"},{"label":"C","text":"Cetirizine"},{"label":"D","text":"Terfenadine (p. 139)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2e2521f3666b","stem":"HT, anticholinergic, sedative and appetite stimu- lating properties","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Promethazine"},{"label":"B","text":"Terfenadine"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyproheptadine"},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxyzine (p. 139, 149)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f62b46717b0f","stem":"11 The conventional H1 antihistaminics possess the following additional properties except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Local anaesthetic"},{"label":"B","text":"Vasopressor"},{"label":"C","text":"Antiarrhythmic"},{"label":"D","text":"Catecholamine potentiating (p. 139, 141)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-71c10c98b319","stem":"12 The capacity of an antihistaminic to produce seda- tion depends on the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relative affinity for central versus periphe- ral H1 receptors"},{"label":"B","text":"Ability to penetrate blood-brain barrier"},{"label":"C","text":"Individual susceptibility"},{"label":"D","text":"Ratio of H1:H2 blockade produced by the drug (p. 139)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"p